WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.940
Two variants both increase one channel's current.

2
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:08.460
Can the neurons do opposite things?

3
00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:10.950
The tempting rule is simple.

4
00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:13.630
More sodium current means more pain.

5
00:00:14.633 --> 00:00:20.993
Nav1.9 breaks it. Its activation and inactivation curves overlap near rest.

6
00:00:22.017 --> 00:00:27.217
Near rest, activation and availability overlap, so some channels are open at any

7
00:00:27.217 --> 00:00:30.897
moment. The population carries a standing inward current.

8
00:00:31.917 --> 00:00:33.077
It is not a spike.

9
00:00:33.957 --> 00:00:36.977
It is a steady depolarizing pull on the resting potential.

10
00:00:38.017 --> 00:00:42.597
Okuda and colleagues made knock-in mice carrying the variant R222S.

11
00:00:43.567 --> 00:00:48.827
That variant is found in familial episodic pain, and their sensory neurons showed

12
00:00:48.827 --> 00:00:50.247
increased evoked firing.

13
00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:55.947
Huang and colleagues expressed two human variants in small rat sensory neurons.

14
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.780
Those two variants are associated with insensitivity to pain.

15
00:01:01.833 --> 00:01:05.993
Both depolarized the neurons and impaired action-potential generation.

16
00:01:07.083 --> 00:01:11.563
One of them shifted the average resting potential by about eleven and a half

17
00:01:11.563 --> 00:01:12.563
millivolts.

18
00:01:13.033 --> 00:01:14.873
Both are gain of channel function.

19
00:01:15.753 --> 00:01:16.753
One raises firing.

20
00:01:17.613 --> 00:01:22.393
The other impairs it, and in a minority of the most depolarized cells abolishes it.

21
00:01:23.093 --> 00:01:24.093
Which should you expect?

22
00:01:30.067 --> 00:01:32.087
You cannot answer from the current alone.

23
00:01:32.967 --> 00:01:37.027
You need the availability of the neuron's Nav1.7-like channels.

24
00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.560
Put the shift on the curve, and the cost shows.

25
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:43.240
Availability falls roughly four-fold.

26
00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:46.480
The size depends on the midpoint assumed.

27
00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:48.520
The direction does not.

28
00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:53.480
Nav1.8 sits on its own curve, far more depolarized.

29
00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:58.720
So treat this as a rough, back-of-envelope argument rather than a measurement.

30
00:01:59.783 --> 00:02:03.723
A small depolarization brings the membrane closer to threshold.

31
00:02:04.663 --> 00:02:07.563
With availability to spare, the neuron can fire more.

32
00:02:08.533 --> 00:02:12.073
A larger one takes away the current the upstroke is built from.

33
00:02:13.017 --> 00:02:17.817
Only a minority, the cells that depolarized furthest, stopped firing altogether.

34
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.000
So what decides the outcome in the neuron?

35
00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:31.520
The outcome depends jointly on the size and time course of the depolarization,

36
00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:37.160
subtype-specific availability, channel densities, the other conductances and the

37
00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:40.860
stimulation. The channel gains, and the neuron can lose.

38
00:02:44.283 --> 00:02:48.623
Starting availability was not measured and matched between these two preparations.

39
00:02:49.767 --> 00:02:54.827
So read this as an account that reconciles two results, not as a measured crossover.

40
00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:57.570
Keep the shape of this argument.

41
00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:03.290
A channel measurement identifies a channel effect; the neuronal context determines

42
00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:04.590
its effect on firing.
